Workstream 2 — Build state + persistence
Type (build.ts): add a build-level map, mirroring attackChains/slotOrder:


/** Per-proc control overrides, keyed `${internalName}:${slotIndex}`. */
procOverrides?: Record<string, {
  enabled: boolean;          // default true
  mode: 'auto' | 'stacks' | 'hp';
  stacks?: number;           // when mode==='stacks'
  hpPct?: number;            // when mode==='hp' (0–100)
}>;
mode:'auto' = expected-uptime default; the map only stores entries the user has touched (absent key = enabled + auto).

Store (buildStore.ts): add actions setProcOverride(internalName, slotIndex, patch) and clearProcOverride(...); prune entries when a slot/power is removed (same spots that filter slotOrder, e.g. :1511).

Serialization (build-serialization.ts): add procOverrides to SlimBuildData (line 290 in build.ts) and to the slimBuild return (:113); restore it in hydrateBuild. Whole-object share/export (url-build-sync.ts) then carries it automatically. It's build-identity data, so it stays in the share (not in the dropped set).

Workstream 3 — Expected-uptime calc (the core model)
New helper in proc-data.ts, reusing existing primitives:


// Expected concurrent stacks at steady state (Little's law):
//   arrivalsPerSec = calculateProcsPerMinute(...) / 60
//   expectedStacks = min(maxStacks, arrivalsPerSec × duration)
calculateProcsPerMinute(ppm, baseRecharge, castTime, radius, enhRecharge, arc) (:2671) already exists; I pass the host power's recharge/cast/geometry (same inputs applyBuildUpProcBonuses gathers at :2638) and the slot's enhanced recharge.

Resolved contribution per proc:

auto + stacks → value × expectedStacks
auto + scaling → floor value (value) — the honest passive baseline
stacks override → value × stacks
hp override → interpolate value…valueMax at hpPct
non-variable, enabled → full value (globals like Steadfast are genuinely always-on)
Workstream 4 — Calc integration (character-totals.ts)
The two gates that currently drop these procs:

:2471 — chance < 1 skip: replace with "skip only if the per-proc override resolves to 0 / disabled." An enabled variable proc now contributes its resolved value.
PPM path is Auto/Toggle-only (:2518) — MotT lives in attacks. Route variable procs through the new resolver regardless of power type, keyed by their procOverrides entry.
Gate precedence: per-proc override wins; the global category toggle (isProcCategoryEnabled) becomes the default when a proc has no explicit override. Rule-of-5 tracking unchanged (shared BonusTracking). Breakdown rows label the source and show stacks/HP% so the dashboard tooltip explains the number.

